#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083243 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083055 :question/id #uuid "148e5378-9177-59ba-9b1e-3633c940babd" :question/math-academy-id "e-596" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=te^t, \\quad y=e^{-t}, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t) = e^t + te^t = (1+t)e^t, \\qquad y'(t)=-e^{-t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{-t}}{(1+t){e}^{t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+t){e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2(1)}(1+1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04488a59-6ffe-5356-ac3f-e74eeb54d1e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288061} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8997e3-1fe3-58c6-8171-ece7ebae387b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{5{e}^{4}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288312} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed485ea-7355-5812-b046-45c3a1d3e1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{4{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288892} {:answer/id #uuid "d60a4cd8-4221-5930-822d-5407abdc3f5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289452} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f5de57-f5aa-5c96-938a-b8c5217f9660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f5de57-f5aa-5c96-938a-b8c5217f9660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289665} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7251d58-9b9c-5089-ac38-6e385106aa2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290193}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f252b1a9-57b3-57ad-97f5-a61e75b56356" :question/math-academy-id "q-20991" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t}}{4{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{2{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-4{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=5,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}(5-1)}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2{e}^{5}\\cdot 4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{8{e}^{5}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16dfb55b-8af4-596f-a315-353765522560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333194} {:answer/id #uuid "c16a839e-1b3e-54f0-9222-740ef19de000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334665} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d5d8b0-70e4-5f14-b80f-590b782f7d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334729} {:answer/id #uuid "ce913bb5-7127-5bc9-acac-45bf128689d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334790} {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e422dcc5-4234-593b-8da3-2f0fe52681a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334976} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e0065-d183-5d46-90dd-110f8b159fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335474}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a73a45f9-70e6-5c98-9255-49149400966d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6808" :question/problem "![](images/17/1739317bf1b1c567f74dccf5b24a1b1dde8eb101d20ceec9652f604dc6f84543.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /6} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8ce46e-daf7-55c0-89df-337038a09ab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236422} {:answer/id #uuid "68ed4719-9dea-5337-9667-8f3e5657f5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236480} {:answer/id #uuid "7f133e8a-78f1-5235-b7a3-a3e3ba78ba25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236604} {:answer/id #uuid "d944ae68-297d-5663-a79f-21d577d3bda0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d5e141-e738-5b2a-a703-6da0a982dde0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236350} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a55434-2f0c-5beb-95aa-d1250e59d512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237512}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "962d8665-19cf-5669-bc0c-46b711cf96ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-102922" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{12},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0854b8df-7a7a-57f6-b376-2a218e36ff2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235854} {:answer/id #uuid "6acdc17f-072f-5f68-86af-7572c7fd6f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236490} {:answer/id #uuid "8afcd281-1318-594a-a9ba-52f65fa8ef56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236686} {:answer/id #uuid "bf44f6a5-6c3e-5582-ba6b-2ec5a73ba371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237032} {:answer/id #uuid "e72bd93b-5cde-5f30-8245-c6eb24cfb3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf44f6a5-6c3e-5582-ba6b-2ec5a73ba371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237032} :answer-field/id #uuid "067447ca-a94b-5969-ab12-e75c926f9806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237450}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f469ae9f-d511-5b2e-b179-c38dadb0919b" :question/math-academy-id "q-102940" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,y=3{t}^{5}+{t}^{3},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}(5{t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}(5{t}^{2}+1)}{4t}=\\frac{3t(5{t}^{2}+1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3(1)(5(1)^{2}+1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} {:answer/id #uuid "4a01b9e0-c4f0-5704-a712-09144764bfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236292} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2e0013-e151-51e9-ad4a-bbb0f6be0b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237009} {:answer/id #uuid "c954ba9f-6430-5221-9f47-5bf333b81728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237094} {:answer/id #uuid "f813ee90-74dd-524e-9db4-b206cc8dc2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12419a49-8a72-5d5a-b25b-542fc5dab0b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235925} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a48016-4413-5cfa-822b-c23d4158c7f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237595}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7720b821-7c21-51f2-9679-46c0e6df53a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-102941" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\frac{4}{\\pi }{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{8}{\\pi }t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{\\pi }t)}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2})}{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fb0b4b-0bf1-5c76-8696-c067c4ad8969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272604} {:answer/id #uuid "6347253c-9fd4-5926-94fe-94155ab5318b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273242} {:answer/id #uuid "722f7007-9e15-5b79-bcc7-366580ece601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273367} {:answer/id #uuid "880d64e6-f8cb-55ff-8d8e-f7e554ac9ca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273554} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c124b-193b-5381-9981-884d4b7bd866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "722f7007-9e15-5b79-bcc7-366580ece601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273367} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5e582ec-54ef-57eb-ade9-5e1ede765e24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d08529b-8573-5553-9a9b-ff64d66c71ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-15457" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,y={cos}^{2}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346e81fc-49e8-5206-8118-642a76aff3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272854} {:answer/id #uuid "848dbf29-ba73-58ac-a73a-39bdd77f8bdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{8e}{27}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273525} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca28c54-4cd9-59d9-8637-13eeddcf1cc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{9e}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273601} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf5f8f4-d801-50ab-a06f-979e93d31278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273740} {:answer/id #uuid "a64af6bb-9a4f-5bcd-ac79-4944cc823d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{8e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346e81fc-49e8-5206-8118-642a76aff3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272854} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a335fd5-65a0-5d78-ae55-0404e4850f92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274906}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2399836b-91ff-571b-9534-908c753b0355" :question/math-academy-id "q-15475" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{1/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}},\\,1<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}{e}^{-1/t}}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}{e}^{-1/2}}{({2}^{2}-1)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{3}^{3/2}\\sqrt{e}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6edb1b-f7b7-5718-8cda-0dd5a71a50be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272774} {:answer/id #uuid "2d253935-f522-5f06-9ee4-61b8bb0a1972" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272789} {:answer/id #uuid "2e13d3fc-0795-5608-98ad-09cff47ca45a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272799} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2e7d1f-a9f1-5d66-a415-8d8c4115d124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273303} {:answer/id #uuid "de45196d-d0c1-55a1-8019-f950885e3562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b6edb1b-f7b7-5718-8cda-0dd5a71a50be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272774} :answer-field/id #uuid "f570cb94-97be-59e0-a398-fb3eb82d9321" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275067}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "18a41cc2-6786-5c7c-b54f-7a6c8dbb881f" :question/math-academy-id "q-15476" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})},\\,y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({e}^{t}),\\,-\\infty <t<1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}, \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}⁡({e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-{e}^{t}{csc}^{2}⁡({e}^{t})}{(\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})}}{{sin}^{2}⁡({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}⁡({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{t})}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{sin}^{3/2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3/2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\,\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837b7697-38dc-5ec2-a5e1-0f7eef16059a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318009} :answer-field/id #uuid "35e83485-026d-51c3-a54b-33ed9e867b92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a64ef463-d187-5f34-bc23-8e25a68dd5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-348303" :question/problem "![](images/59/59f053b9a122018d78cd9c30f5a53cd67cd2ff645d02ccb6d311e6183e081102.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+2{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+4t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56511671-1b56-5091-a324-56b442d1f811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319644} :answer-field/id #uuid "88549d85-6690-5559-9bdd-e3b23c490bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320444}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d2c31a-1162-5af9-962c-a704ed1fd8ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-353350" :question/problem "![](images/ab/abab926e465522d30b236577bf8a1654502807b864c9fed8a4cebb84efffd3a2.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "397cc35d-3f47-519e-b5bf-73d3a8b311b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319568} :answer-field/id #uuid "6920c4ef-fcdf-5331-9ff1-0d0ddf66b7c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320382}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6474a49e-c58a-508a-9e50-a787079f364a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353351" :question/problem "![](images/35/35c62ccf48c4d0a49729da04b443cb94d112e673e0c24e7784d22f48eaabf8f7.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /3} & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a52de76-716b-5d41-98a9-3943b7c59241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321352} :answer-field/id #uuid "abecd077-f709-5b82-9c40-6c4dc262a11d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae92af54-aa04-5ea8-bfcd-575703012570" :question/math-academy-id "q-363490" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{3/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{3/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3({1}^{2}+4)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3(5)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15\\sqrt{5}\\,{e}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{75{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e634f729-6fad-5eee-8c7f-dd8fad161e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e634f729-6fad-5eee-8c7f-dd8fad161e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368396} :answer-field/id #uuid "01554cb6-b048-5d22-9b7e-6a54c35e86d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3fd0997f-50c1-5e89-b401-28e2788c518b" :question/math-academy-id "q-348305" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{t}},\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{3t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=4.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{-3/2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t}}{9{t}^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(t-1)}{3{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-6{t}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{t}(t-1)}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=4,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,4} & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}(4-1)}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{e}^{4}\\cdot 3}{2} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72ee5fc9-0606-5e80-bb6e-fc3422758a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72ee5fc9-0606-5e80-bb6e-fc3422758a8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368472} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7c6cf65-f4d1-540f-8883-933b5cb9cf59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368471}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "282535b6-7f0b-5681-8ed7-3e9b8413bd06" :question/math-academy-id "q-353363" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{2/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}},\\,0<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}})}{(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{2/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2/t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2({1}^{2}+1)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(2)^{3/2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e807c1b4-ec12-578e-adfe-38f82bc92701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368630} {:answer/id #uuid "12e80468-7314-5375-a06c-ac6bbe01fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368631} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4fa272-e3a0-5a72-966e-a6949699eb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368632} {:answer/id #uuid "bbab7461-db2d-5d59-b338-baad2f7e5541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368633} {:answer/id #uuid "584d0dc4-bafb-53d6-8b06-244aa54043ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12e80468-7314-5375-a06c-ac6bbe01fde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368631} :answer-field/id #uuid "6802ba7f-a62f-596f-82ab-3508a6bdc4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368629}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c3b2996-4d45-5fac-b139-960f69bf9ec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-6730" :question/problem "![](images/94/9434dda63c08fc1202d16ab5410fe7061050515876ffa473d8720db39079af3e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bead1cb0-40bc-5682-8aea-b1306552b221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401064} {:answer/id #uuid "86056585-0e06-5b86-b0c0-36b6f0375c43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401065} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15ff06-b974-57e0-9d0d-91476444df9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401066} {:answer/id #uuid "552b9d8c-6c80-5fa5-9717-bf514c6bbbca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401067} {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a93180-72f9-5046-97f5-9cb178f53496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401068} :answer-field/id #uuid "4182a726-9587-5280-b0ae-9a47c4e9e265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16fc9f31-33f6-5972-9529-3afa5f1e1dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-15465" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60199c4-dc25-5568-af40-de39cd26589d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401071} {:answer/id #uuid "41c812bd-cb3b-5fc5-aef0-fb585602e943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401072} {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} {:answer/id #uuid "a5fc02f7-9666-528e-9191-ebdcde2135fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401074} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea8ed64-c406-5164-b57d-847952b47323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae37cc4a-dbda-5fe8-9ffa-a6b2a950bdbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401073} :answer-field/id #uuid "afbc7c1c-c03b-5382-bd6b-5a9beec491d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9e0331-ffe6-5489-b436-faf1f0bea590" :question/math-academy-id "q-15469" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}+1,\\,y={t}^{4}+2{t}^{2},\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t({t}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{4t({t}^{2}+1)}{6{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({t}^{2}+1)}{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2((1)^{2}+1)}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531 :topic/math-academy-id 798 :topic/title "Differentiating Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6a1fb9-a35a-5b64-913e-c9df98bccbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{24}{11}\\,x+\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333430} {:answer/id #uuid "53eed6ee-9b5a-505e-be78-f6bb4c4f4050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333753} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} {:answer/id #uuid "95bef049-706d-5788-8e10-cb68b22507f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334321} {:answer/id #uuid "a6894270-0960-537c-a6f0-979b8b3809d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac34767-b922-5f65-a668-1c1e1e6c8f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335447}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94e72be-f23a-535e-b719-cf664553611b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6857" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f56a41ed-59a1-5429-9c88-30a68cc3b4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{3}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082410} {:answer/id #uuid "0a46d561-b2f3-58d4-94a4-01099db3bb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082411} {:answer/id #uuid "1068a7ae-c66e-5103-a69c-cd043a388acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082412} {:answer/id #uuid "383e37b1-7785-5316-993d-06f2ecc4bf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082413} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3d43fe-583d-5c3c-b161-e97547fb6627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 = \\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082414}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dce6874e-77a5-5de0-abb5-e4403a900937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082409}] :question/id #uuid "c6d49020-9a4a-5e6f-8526-58e27d438cd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-6862" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{2} - 2t, y = t^{5}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t =-1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2483de-bb9b-57f7-a79f-34885e11205d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253803} {:answer/id #uuid "c65aceae-e84b-5651-9ab5-ee308d3662ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb258778-9d8d-556c-8e5b-b5614a8787a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255476} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0c7cf4-bb80-57c1-a9c1-3db0dee8005d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d4061c-5bca-5874-a889-0e5b0bcb0553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "405e30d2-1994-59ac-94e7-5e7998f82913" :question/math-academy-id "q-122698" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d7761-9781-53c7-9bbc-78084d322e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{9},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272450} {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a5947-d436-56d5-880b-0465859f1964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{7},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273188} {:answer/id #uuid "80184777-1859-51fd-907f-4bb3c159a4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273486} {:answer/id #uuid "e955007e-17f7-5db4-9fbf-cecc47ec6559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} :answer-field/id #uuid "affbb4e4-fcfc-5f64-b6b5-e473fd4f7c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274941}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51cd744a-a0db-52ff-b937-07f4a84310d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15564" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287acad-245f-58bd-be4f-6dbce3ede11e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318564}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9181afbc-9550-51c6-b28c-f82450210472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348109" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbbd214-a055-53f2-b3b5-67bd9aed6a47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee152229-0df5-5943-83fa-bcee1ecff76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a109daf-d742-5b15-87da-27961e643b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083057 :question/id #uuid "db8c5a47-27b8-548d-8bd7-65289cff06da" :question/math-academy-id "e-598" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "898e04b8-1b7e-59e7-b03f-3ece54aa093b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/math-academy-id 987 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa64069-1206-5421-8a4f-a6e818653412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080166} {:answer/id #uuid "25e79155-ec81-5750-8024-8b85f305474b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{12}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080167} {:answer/id #uuid "287cb0ff-c745-5fe1-847e-2f4eb5158055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{8}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080168} {:answer/id #uuid "52f88dfd-78ed-5cbe-9062-4303b028313d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{4}{7}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080169} {:answer/id #uuid "571c9ee7-eb34-5dec-8694-3d455d4bf27e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{3}, 0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080170}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c73154-e807-5407-a20e-cfe049bc0a94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080165}] :question/id #uuid "8def6986-b021-50a6-91db-c2da4068ed38" :question/math-academy-id "q-15558" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d40bd1a54762b360dc590f014d1df9731168f8620df07ee8f0294bd078fc8da5.png)\n\nThe curve shown above is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{4} + t, y = t^{2}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t =-1$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5faf98-bf15-5a2b-9fdc-09a9a727c6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273199} {:answer/id #uuid "5f37ca8d-3ea5-5dc1-85e2-3a3270cb4ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273206} {:answer/id #uuid "7e745226-ecb3-5bd0-91a4-532e194cf6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273471} {:answer/id #uuid "818af87a-0a7b-5074-95df-e9381f58f773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1129c14f-0c46-52c4-9b9a-b4200ce4b285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272542} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3ec910-ef36-5928-896c-acb859df3e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274961}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbab7bcf-6a8b-5a33-ae85-61d0acf13fd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15570" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f344a5d-f20b-5d29-bc4b-455872b79c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254087} {:answer/id #uuid "9e617510-b480-5d70-b155-ca6d7883d5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255062} {:answer/id #uuid "b77a05d6-9403-5a91-8f6f-1d9bf6e028b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255297} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa266e3-b7f3-5389-b0e2-d0701e510855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255519} {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0067f8f-a0ec-5d6c-b01d-2fd5c88ca389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255523} :answer-field/id #uuid "38fc7afa-faeb-58be-bde2-8b46b42e1581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45f7632b-4b69-541a-a152-af5eeab4cd2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122898" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9706bfa8-3bb2-557b-b07a-3cb1417d9a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318080} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c918410-e361-5bcd-9d06-8fb2ed574022" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9f0baf7-6810-54d5-a562-b853fd4577c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318344} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d59423d-64dc-523d-9ab7-74063ac68307" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "12a180e2-5235-5f82-b761-d705d49812b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-350386" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a237665-5e72-5ef0-972a-daed0f365535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318093} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e4c9a-9cfb-50e6-a993-4807d39b6109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24461eb9-a35c-56e6-b177-bda2a062fa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e11efaf-e900-5155-8422-a40a0e3088ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe83c43c-ad52-5fa5-817f-abc0952cf819" :question/math-academy-id "q-350395" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7{t}^{2}-9t,\\,y={t}^{6}+2{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7({1}^{2})-9(1)=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{6}+2({1}^{2})=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,3).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14t-9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{5}+4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{5}+4t}{14t-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{6({1}^{5})+4(1)}{14(1)-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} {:answer/id #uuid "8002ae58-b098-5bda-ad00-0c7449da285b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce2a6a-ee62-5f62-9806-39eacf4d3091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334202} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd1a297-06d6-51de-b3b8-df8da09b3b58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334652} {:answer/id #uuid "ea795a17-22c8-5438-a337-67f964713d05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125bc6a6-fb74-5cce-8504-caeb0db01da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333153} :answer-field/id #uuid "48542b45-fcfe-5b1a-aede-206d84c3eeaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dafa44c9-9831-51c8-95fc-4a16466f716d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6858" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{1}{3})\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac6379c3-3e1c-5a08-ae54-d99f43489aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400067} {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc9efa8-1c48-5867-a1e9-734559b5450d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{2e}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400069} {:answer/id #uuid "f85a9aab-60c9-5ec0-8bc2-0588e38d3687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400070} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e29ac-c571-528d-b8c7-bb6ee10f19df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400071}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359d0ac8-26db-56f1-98c6-ec3481c97cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e41e50-ea1d-54b4-8438-62f4c2b9fa61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400066}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "138e1f42-3fb6-5013-87c6-eb59c3a912d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-122931" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2t{e}^{t}-2{e}^{t},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e, \\\\ y & =2(1){e}^{1}-2{e}^{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(e,0).$  For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t{e}^{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =2(1)=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,2\\,}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(e,0)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-e) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{e}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5eb255d-3cfa-5ae7-b5a0-ab9f9f6b8e45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403824} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb81c4-43fa-5409-88e4-4f5ef499dd13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403825} {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} {:answer/id #uuid "9555e468-7e78-5b3e-afb9-8e76c044cd31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403827} {:answer/id #uuid "13841314-a76c-5620-906a-428ce704c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e0722a-6252-5355-b6bd-65afbf34c242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403826} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc1b61e-03fa-5261-ae12-a9f784057a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403823}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f065fb78-5c08-5a67-9bfa-56a34450ed2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75288" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Normal Line to a Parametric Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083074 :question/id #uuid "72076ddb-4a3a-530f-854c-7b1400cd9859" :question/math-academy-id "e-6240" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0fa5cb-58b2-5991-aeb3-3c72630b91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080742} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080743} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb6ae8-5736-5ecb-a2b0-06e71866c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{3π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080744} {:answer/id #uuid "1f118b88-363d-549d-8d78-43f7f7a3fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080745} {:answer/id #uuid "416acf6e-914c-5f39-8409-93bf2a0d33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080746}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b486e99f-74f6-58c2-873a-74bcf30e0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177fc0fe-e31a-5f41-9aab-356a0006fb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401861} {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401862} {:answer/id #uuid "1843433f-102c-52fd-970b-a78c3d6b3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401863} {:answer/id #uuid "50097778-e660-56b2-929d-8baced007acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401864} {:answer/id #uuid "835e0688-6998-5bbb-863e-83fa6322632b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10683790-7483-51aa-9bcd-7831b9d3d041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401862} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9d95bd4-f167-53f1-8c45-da39995abe7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ecc25d-c92a-5600-9145-4a0ef2f4de03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080748} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1abc77-9bfa-5b8c-b83e-648cc0c2209d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080749} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8a2573-0097-5b71-8d97-b01308919ce1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080750} {:answer/id #uuid "3790a990-007e-51c6-ac63-c8e6c774c409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080751} {:answer/id #uuid "54fd89e0-a522-5183-9571-f9c76c9a09ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080752}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a95819-b401-5a37-808b-da72270a670b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080747}] :question/id #uuid "5d756ec3-8e62-5cb5-9a5c-591a8f7912fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-21206" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t - 1, y = t^{3} - 2, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a045771-d8ae-5cdc-84d3-b3cd816ec00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254044} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb229ef-4966-5f1f-9ed2-fcb9e90d768f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254060} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b04d28-4dff-524a-8690-c15be0f1788a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255112} {:answer/id #uuid "d162cfde-5987-5fd8-bdf5-7c6350a3be39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255538} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3d2d1-db33-5eba-a8dd-406ca13f50df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6d3d2d1-db33-5eba-a8dd-406ca13f50df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255830} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3597dd1-c872-5d2d-bc36-073975f4bf30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa6201e7-5014-5e6b-8fe5-7f2f7e237473" :question/math-academy-id "q-122726" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4{t}^{5},\\,y=9t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n20{t}^{4}=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(0)=9\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} {:answer/id #uuid "5833d2bf-74d0-5ea2-94bc-f893f8489ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285598} {:answer/id #uuid "98173c7f-8ba4-5f06-88fe-3425d308e469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286180} {:answer/id #uuid "ab587a99-40a5-5596-90ea-b5a5e96c6204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286350} {:answer/id #uuid "e27e9371-7143-528d-820d-b073e0dc0b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02788049-040e-5c76-86e2-58bb6a5bbbef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284808} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6ee0e5-3926-5962-8761-13aa6562ef64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287282}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6e05e4c-c6f0-521f-b67c-d81eff855827" :question/math-academy-id "q-20853" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fed31d-5bfd-56fb-b736-1523716f3623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288242} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a45a52-bb1d-5298-8eea-b0736136a858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289203} {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} {:answer/id #uuid "d48c86fe-0944-5271-85f8-d01acc9ec550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289442} {:answer/id #uuid "df29cd35-a94b-54f6-a005-8167cc54c05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b35e4f36-00ed-5382-b39e-4e0a8616fde8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=0$ and $t=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289215} :answer-field/id #uuid "edae3f4a-489e-5181-a3c8-af691c065008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290225}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14df7ed3-c243-590c-b10b-e1904947b459" :question/math-academy-id "q-21072" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0,\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=0$ and $t=\\pi .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12952b5e-889f-54cc-a122-5d50215660f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "162eb28c-9915-5e71-854b-15570d6140d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc186f89-e80b-5eb4-a0f9-50d2a3804e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318301} :answer-field/id #uuid "45248884-e4ee-5dd3-b87d-9707883c9d1c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca39289-4112-5812-a40f-16d38e719123" :question/math-academy-id "q-350359" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e270e8d4-fbf0-5120-8d17-e3b700621933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318320} :answer-field/id #uuid "57214c85-b840-55fd-b641-4b1c74936c23" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23a8be49-ec43-55d3-af29-85069b1bbe66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a3120f3-3da1-51f8-aa50-545e1dd0fd1e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318725}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa66657-dfb2-58cb-a875-a940d0ba53d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-350368" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323713 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acc15cc8-16fd-50fa-8523-a77c71d90858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322500} :answer-field/id #uuid "2032fb96-09c8-5ded-b5bd-deeda3577ddf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323229} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d38fff03-6da9-5ce6-a633-7885919027e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322799} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0b201f2-c9f2-5028-a916-abafa9097eaa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323645}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "587316a5-f847-5f83-912c-b52ebafdaeb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-357255" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}]}
